    "body": "Norfolk Southern reported higher third-quarter earnings and record revenue Thursday as freight volume grew and fuel surcharges increased, although expenses consumed a larger share of revenue.\nThe railroad (NYSE: NSC ) said revenue rose 14%, to $3.5 billion, on a 3% increase in volume. Higher fuel surcharges accounted for eight percentage points of revenue growth. Income from railway operations increased 5%, to approximately $1.2 billion, while diluted earnings per share rose 7%, to $3.37.\nAdjusted earnings per share increased 10%, to $3.62, excluding merger-related expenses and effects of the East Palestine derailment, along with restructuring and other charges in the year-earlier comparison. Adjusted operating income rose 7%, to approximately $1.2 billion.\nDespite the earnings growth, Norfolk Southern’s operating ratio, a key indicator of efficiency, worsened 2.8 percentage points, to 67.4%. Its adjusted operating ratio increased 2.1 points, to 65.4%, underscoring pressure on margins even as revenue reached a quarterly record.\nChief Executive Mark George said productivity improvements helped earnings, while tighter trucking capacity increased rail’s appeal to shippers.\n“Demand for rail service increased during the quarter, as a tighter trucking market highlighted rail’s competitive advantages and underscored the opportunity for a more efficient, capacity-enhanced freight network,” George said in the earnings announcement.\n“We remain focused on investing in a stronger, more resilient rail network that can support growth we see on the horizon,” he said.\nWhy it matters: Norfolk Southern is benefiting from a tighter trucking market, but stronger freight demand is not translating into better margins.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nCSX profit jumps on volume and revenue growth\nNew York short line wins $11.2 million 